# Data-flow discipline

> **Auto-distributed via simpl_knowledge. Propose edits with a PR on `simpl-techs/simpl_knowledge`.**

This skill is **always-on**. It governs the shape and direction of data as it moves through the stack. The universal principles and the routing table live in the `coding-standards` hub. Not covered here:
- Where code lives, including layered packaging and where DTOs (shared layer) and resolvers sit: `architecture-discipline`.
- Transactions, async, retry budgets, observability: `state-and-persistence`.
- Test structure, AAA, mocking, asserting on state and end-to-end shape: `testing-policy` (it repeats the fixture-data rules below as a fixture-design cue).

## Typed data through the stack

- **Thread typed data through the call graph; do not re-extract it from formatted output.** A value that exists as a structured field at one layer is carried as a structured field to every layer that needs it. Parsing it back out of a rendered string is fragile and lossy, and signals that a typed field was dropped one layer up.
- **Add the field rather than infer it.** Give the downstream component a kwarg or DTO attribute. Reconstructing data the upstream already had is a smell that always grows worse over time.
- **Rendered output is terminal.** Once a value is serialized for display, transport or model consumption, it never flows back into structured logic; everything structured must exist before the one-way render step.

## Single-source state resolution

- **Resolve a derived value exactly once, upstream; consume it strictly downstream.** Exactly one function, the resolver, owns the decision for a field. Everything else takes its verdict as input and obeys it: it never re-derives it or sniffs the underlying signals again. Why: two functions computing the same value are guaranteed to disagree under some input, and the layers drift silently; the bug only shows up in a runtime trace.
- **Resolution priority is explicit, ordered and in one place.** When signals compete (real-time input, stored state, hard constraints), the priority order sits at the top of a single function, not in `if/else` branches across modules, so a reader can point at one location and read the rule end-to-end.
- **Hard constraints cap candidates; they do not replace them.** A constraint set narrows what the resolver may pick but does not produce the verdict. The candidate signal and the constraint set are distinct inputs to the resolver and stay distinct in code.

## Validation layering

- **Validation is owned by the boundary that can enforce it deterministically.** A structural rule (format, length, type) checkable at a single seam belongs to that seam, and the other layers trust its guarantee. Concerns repeated across layers always drift, so pick the one layer that gets it right.
- **Do not stack validation behind a final gate.** When a human or an authoritative check approves the record at a later stage, redundant earlier validation is friction that adds no signal. Stack layers only when each catches something the others provably cannot.
- **Allow-lists are scoped to the freshest relevant input.** To check that output corresponds to something the system genuinely received, source the truth from the most recent inbound signal, not the cumulative history: older context is noise and lets stale matches through.

## Output and DTO invariants

- **Validate the fields of an emitted object together, as a value.** Related fields constrain each other, so the invariant lives on the object, not on each field: write the check as one function that takes the whole object and rejects incoherent combinations. **Coherence of an emitted value is a code-level invariant** with the weight of a foreign-key constraint or an enum check, enforced before the value leaves the producing function, not delegated to "if the consumer cares".
- **Sanitize forbidden output at the serialization seam, not via upstream instructions.** When a consumer renders a value verbatim and some characters or shapes are forbidden, enforce that at the boundary that produces the wire format. Asking the producing layer (especially a non-deterministic one) to "just not emit them" is the wrong layer; only the boundary enforces reliably.

## Failure surfacing

- **Internal failures escalate; they never end in a silent no-op.** A failure handled only by a log line and a no-action return is silently discarded: the entity looks healthy in production, no retry path picks it up, no operator sees it. The two acceptable outcomes are a **bounded retry** (explicit attempt cap and an error signal per attempt; see `state-and-persistence`) or a **structured handoff** to a reviewer queue.
- **Logging is not surfacing.** Log lines, chat-channel pings and metrics are observability and produce no follow-up action. Surfacing means the failure lands in a persistent row, queue or status that a retry path or a human will actually pick up.
- **Exception: a cost that could not be recorded is not the work's failure.** The work succeeded, so it never raises, retries the work or blocks. It is reported on the cost-tracking channel, and provider reconciliation is the persistent record that catches what the channel misses (`state-and-persistence` → Observability).
- **Escalation outputs obey the same invariants as primary outputs.** A handoff payload (review request, retry record, error envelope) carries the same coherence guarantees as a normal DTO. "Something went wrong, decide for me" is offloading, not surfacing.

## Test and fixture data discipline

- **Real production data is confined to replay tests.** Real names, identifiers, contact details and UUIDs do not appear in prompts, unit tests or shared fixtures. The carve-out is replay tests whose explicit purpose is to reproduce a historical production trace; anonymizing them would erase their meaning.
- **Generic example data is obviously fake:** use values that cannot be mistaken for real ones (e.g. `example.com` domains, zero-padded numbers, all-zero UUIDs). Anonymized-real values are worse than obviously fake ones because they pass eyeballing and hide PII leaks.
